Files and directories through their POSIX calls, one wrapper per call, each returning a
`ca::result`. Paths are NUL-terminated `const char*`; descriptors are plain `int`s the caller
closes; buffers are spans, so a `char` array or any contiguous container passes directly.

| Group | Functions |
|---|---|
| open and close | `open`, `openat`, `close`, `dup`, `mkstemp`, `fcntl` |
| data | `read`, `write`, `write_all`, `pread`, `pwrite`, `seek`, `truncate`, `ftruncate`, `fallocate`, `fsync`, `fdatasync` |
| status | `stat`, `lstat`, `fstat`, `fstatat`, `access`, `faccessat` (a `file_status`, a `file_kind`) |
| names | `rename`, `rename_noreplace`, `renameat`, `link`, `linkat`, `symlink`, `symlinkat`, `readlink`, `readlinkat`, `realpath`, `unlink`, `unlinkat` |
| directories | `mkdir`, `mkdirat`, `mkdtemp`, `rmdir`, `opendir`, `fdopendir`, `readdir`, `dirfd`, `closedir`, `chdir`, `getcwd` |
| ownership | `chmod`, `fchmod`, `chown`, `fchown`, `lchown`, `umask`, `utimens` |

The flags of `open` are the raw values of the `ca::fs` options (`fs::access::READ_ONLY`,
`fs::create_t::OPEN_VALUE`, ...), defined without `<fcntl.h>`; `fs::descriptor` (layer 6)
combines them as typed options instead, and closes the descriptor for you.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	char dir[] = "example_XXXXXX";
	if (!ca::sys::mkdtemp(dir))
		return 1;
	auto here = ca::sys::openat(ca::sys::at::cwd, dir, ca::fs::access::READ_ONLY | ca::fs::directory_t::OPEN_VALUE, 0U);
	if (!here)
		return 2;

	constexpr int create = ca::fs::access::READ_WRITE | ca::fs::create_t::OPEN_VALUE | ca::fs::exclusive_t::OPEN_VALUE
		| ca::fs::cloexec_t::OPEN_VALUE;
	auto fd = ca::sys::openat(*here, "notes.txt", create, 0644U);
	if (!fd || !ca::sys::write_all(*fd, ca::sv::str{"first line\nsecond line\n"}))
		return 3;

	char word[6]{};
	auto got = ca::sys::pread(*fd, ca::span<char>{word}.prefix(6U), 11U);
	if (!got || ca::sv::str{word, *got} != ca::sv::str{"second"})
		return 4;
	auto status = ca::sys::fstat(*fd);
	if (!status || status->size() != 23U || status->kind() != ca::sys::file_kind::regular)
		return 5;
	auto end = ca::sys::seek(*fd, 0, ca::sys::seek_origin::end);
	if (!end || *end != 23 || !ca::sys::close(*fd))
		return 6;

	auto again = ca::sys::openat(*here, "notes.txt", create, 0644U);
	if (again || again.error() != ca::errc::exists)
		return 7;

	auto listing = ca::sys::opendir(dir);
	if (!listing)
		return 8;
	ca::usz entries = 0U;
	for (auto name = ca::sys::readdir(*listing); name && !name->empty(); name = ca::sys::readdir(*listing))
		++entries;
	if (entries != 1U || !ca::sys::closedir(*listing))
		return 9;

	if (!ca::sys::unlinkat(*here, "notes.txt", 0) || !ca::sys::close(*here))
		return 10;
	return ca::sys::rmdir(dir) ? 0 : 11;
}
```

### Details worth knowing

- `read`, `write`, `pread`, `pwrite`, `open` and `ftruncate` retry on EINTR. `read` returns 0 only at
  end of file; `write` may write less than asked, `write_all` loops until everything is written
  (and on failure an unknown prefix was).
- `close` releases the descriptor even when it fails, and counts EINTR as success: retrying could
  close a descriptor another thread was just given.
- `readdir` skips "." and ".." and returns an empty name at the end; the name is valid until the
  next call on the stream.
- `readlink` writes the target unterminated and returns its length; a length equal to the buffer
  size may mean truncation.
- `realpath` returns a block to release with `ca::sys::free`.
- `rename_noreplace` fails with `errc::exists` atomically instead of replacing, where the file
  system supports it.
- The `*at` variants resolve relative paths against an open directory (`sys::at::cwd` for the
  working directory), which avoids races on paths that change underneath; their flags are in
  `sys::at` (`symlink_nofollow`, `remove_directory`, ...).
- `file_status` gives times in nanoseconds since the epoch, permissions as the 07777 bits, and the
  type as a `file_kind`.
